  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and radar and satellite imagery show \nstrong high pressure centered over northwest Ontario and extending\nsouth across the western Great Lakes early this morning. Weak \ncold advection is occurring, with patchy ground fog developing \nwhere skies are clear. However, this fog will be short-lived early\nthis morning as mid and high clouds thicken from the west. These \nclouds are already moving into the region ahead of shortwave \nenergy and broad warm advection occurring over the central and \nnorthern Plains. Showers and thunderstorms are blossoming on the \nnose of a low level jet across northern Nebraska and South Dakota \nin a region of strong moisture convergence and moisture transport \ncoincident with mid-level frontogenesis. This activity is \noccurring ahead of surface low pressure centered over northeast \nColorado, which will be the primary weather driver for the region \nover the next 36 to 48 hours.\n\nHeavy Rainfall Potential Today through Saturday: \nAs this shortwave energy and associated low pressure lift \nnortheast, the potential for heavy rainfall becomes the primary \nfocus of this forecast. The low level jet will continue to lift \nnortheast today, with strong moisture convergence developing on \nthe nose of a 30 to 40 kt low level jet beneath the right rear \nquadrant of an upper jet streak. This will lead to rain spreading \neast from South Dakota across Minnesota and into Wisconsin this \nmorning. The rain will start out light as it enters north-central \nWisconsin by late morning, with intensity increasing this \nafternoon as the nose of the low level jet becomes centered across\ncentral Wisconsin. In general, the band of rain will be weakening\nas it progresses across the region alongside a weakening low \nlevel jet. The rain will continue for much of tonight through \nSaturday and into Saturday night as isentropic ascent and mid- \nlevel frontogenesis continue to be centered across Wisconsin. A \nfew thunderstorms will be possible tonight and Saturday, but no \nsevere weather is expected. \n\nThe signal for the heaviest rainfall continues to shift south and\ngenerally lies from southeast Minnesota to southern Wisconsin. \nThis trend makes sense since this is where the highest \nprecipitable water values near 2 inches, the most unstable CAPE in\nexcess of 1000 j/kg, and a persistent low level jet will be \nlocated. As a result, the risk of flash flooding has decreased \nconsiderably as the rainfall rates do not look supportive. The \nlatest guidance shows the risk of 2 inches of rain in 6 hours is \nunder 5 percent during the peak rainfall period late tonight. \nOverall rainfall amounts from this afternoon through Saturday \nafternoon have decreased to 1 to 2 inches over central and east- \ncentral Wisconsin. Most of the rain will gradually diminish on \nSaturday night.\n\nConfidence in rainfall amounts is generally lower than normal for\nthis time range of the forecast due to uncertainty in the area of\nheaviest rainfall and potential for locally heavy downpours in\nthunderstorms.\n\nQuiet Weather Next Week:\nLooking ahead to next week, shortwave energy originating from the\nwestern North American trough will slowly migrate east from the \ncentral Plains to the southern Great Lakes and eventually the east\ncoast. Therefore, it appears that most of the rain will remain \nsouth of the region. There are only small chances of rain up to 20\nto 30 percent forecast for Monday over parts of central and east-\ncentral Wisconsin. Otherwise, strong high pressure over northern \nOntario and northern Quebec looks to keep ample dry air across \nnorthern Wisconsin. Other than Monday, no precipitation is \nforecast next week.\n\nWith high pressure maintaining dry and quiet conditions for the\nbulk of the extended period, temperatures will be undergoing a\nslow warming trend. Daytime highs ranging from the low to mid 60s\non Monday will gradually warm each day, reaching the low to mid\n70s by Thursday and Friday.",
